Yes. In your `Rounds` model, use the JavaScript/Sequelize attribute names:

```js
indexes: [
  {
    unique: true,
    fields: ["gameId", "roundNumber"],
    name: "one_round_number_per_game",
  },
],
```

That is correct because your model defines:

```js
gameId: {
  field: "game_id",
}

roundNumber: {
  field: "round_number",
}
```

Sequelize uses the attribute names in the model definition and translates them to the SQL column names when it creates the index. Therefore, this will create an index on:

```sql
(game_id, round_number)
```

The mapping is:

```text
JavaScript attribute    SQL column
gameId                  game_id
roundNumber             round_number
```

So:

- Model `indexes.fields`: use `gameId`, `roundNumber`.
- Raw SQL or migration definitions: use `game_id`, `round_number`.
- `references.key`: use the SQL column name, such as `game_id`.

This follows Sequelize’s model-index behavior and its `field` attribute mapping. ([Sequelize indexes](https://sequelize.org/docs/v6/other-topics/indexes/), [Sequelize naming strategies](https://sequelize.org/docs/v6/other-topics/naming-strategies/))
